/* Per-room guides for the six hands-on classrooms: what the room is for,
   what you need, how one session runs. Written for the grown-up who will
   actually sit on the floor — specific to the activities really in the room,
   never generic filler. */
const SUBJECT_GUIDES={
 art:{what:`The Art Studio is about making choices, not perfect pictures: tear or cut coloured paper, arrange it, layer it, then mix your own shades from red, yellow and blue washable paint. Both activities here — Make a shape collage and Mixing Colours — need nothing more than paper, a glue stick and paints you probably already have.`,run:`Spread a table cover first, put out the materials, and let your child lead while you narrate what you notice: “you tore that strip so small”, “the red and yellow made orange”. Every activity has a printable or on-screen sheet with drawing space, so the results can go on the wall, not in the bin.`},
 science:{what:`The Science Lab turns kitchen-table questions into real experiments: a bean growing in a clear cup, objects that sink or float, a week of sky watching, shadows made with a torch. Each one follows the same honest loop — predict, test, write down what actually happened — which is the whole habit of science in one sitting.`,run:`Pick one experiment, gather the short materials list (seeds, a tub of water, a torch — nothing rare), and ask your child to guess out loud before every test. The printable diary sheets give them a place to record results, and “wrong” predictions are the best part: that is where the talking starts.`},
 nature:{what:`Nature Club slows the day down: one safe listening spot for a sound map, a handful of fallen leaves for rubbings, textures to compare and leaf creatures to build. You stay in one place together, and the outside world does the entertaining.`,run:`Choose your spot first (a bench, a patch of grass, an open window on a rainy day), sit still for a few minutes, then draw or rub what you notice. There is no right number of sounds or leaves — the listening diary and compare-two-leaves sheets give the walk a gentle finish without turning it into a lesson.`},
 maths:{what:`The Maths Room makes patterns physical: big paper shapes in two or three colours, arranged into a rule someone else has to find. One activity, Make a repeating pattern, carries the whole room — and pattern-spotting is the root of later number sense, reading rhythm and even code.`,run:`Start a pattern yourself and stop mid-way: “what comes next?” Then swap jobs and let your child build one for you — the challenge-find-my-rule game is where the thinking happens. Cut the printed pattern pieces for small hands, or let them draw their own shapes to break the pattern on purpose.`},
 literacy:{what:`Story Corner is where invented characters get real endings: draw a story map with a beginning, a muddle in the middle and a way out, then tell it out loud before writing a word. The handwriting practice screen sits here too, for tracing letters, numbers and names at your child's own pace.`,run:`Fold a paper into three parts — or print the story map — and fill it together: “who is it? what goes wrong? how does it end?” Telling the story out loud first is the point; writing comes after, and invented spelling is welcome. One map per sitting is plenty.`},
 engineering:{what:`Build & Tinker runs fair tests: two paper bridges, one folded and one flat, stretched between equal stacks of books, loaded with identical blocks until something gives. Predict, build, test, improve — then test again. It is the design loop with nothing but paper.`,run:`Set the stacks up before your child joins, write the prediction down, and count the blocks out loud so the comparison is fair. The design-and-results sheets keep score, and the improve-my-bridge page gives the second test a purpose: beat your own record.`}
};
/* Per-age guides for the activity cupboard: what the range usually looks
   like, what the grown-up does, how to adapt. Every child differs — the
   wording stays a guide, never a milestone checklist. */
const AGE_GUIDES={
 '3-5':{what:`Many children in this range can tear paper, glue, paint with a brush, sort by colour and sit for roughly ten to fifteen minutes of making. Scissors are still a two-adult-hands job for some — every activity here marks cutting as optional and grown-up handled.`,help:`Do the setup, stay close, and let the mess happen: your job is the materials and the chat, not the outcome. Ask “tell me about it” instead of “what is it?”, and stop while it is still fun — two finished minutes beat twenty hurried ones.`},
 '6-8':{what:`This is the hands-on sweet spot: most children here can plan a pattern before building it, keep a simple diary, predict before testing and write their own labels. They still need an adult for water tubs, paints and bridge-loading, but the ideas should be theirs.`,help:`Hand over the pencil: your child predicts, records and improves while you handle anything sharp, wet or heavy. Ask for the rule behind the pattern or the reason the bridge fell — explaining out loud is where the learning sticks.`},
 '9-12':{what:`Older makers want their work taken seriously: fair tests with real variables, stories with an actual plot, patterns with a rule worth hiding. The same activities stretch — a stronger bridge shape, a longer story map, a sound map with a legend — because the thinking, not the materials, scales up.`,help:`Step back to lab-partner distance. Agree the rules of the test together, then let your child run it and defend the results; join in only where safety says so. The write-up pages give them something to be genuinely proud of.`}
};
